 "title": "How to Be More Persuasive Using Storytelling",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that data informs while stories persuade, making storytelling essential for any presentation or pitch. He shares a personal anecdote about a hedge fund broker who walked away from a $60,000 commission to prove that storytelling beats flat statements.",
 "key_points": [
  "Data informs, story persuades.",
  "If you do not have a story, you have no way to persuade people.",
  "Trust is the most valuable currency in business, but stating this flatly is ineffective.",
  "Example story: As a hedge fund trader, the creator gave a new Merrill Lynch broker an order for one million shares of Biogen to test him.",
  "The broker initially told him he would call him back on the outside line because his biotech trader was out and the backup sucked.",
  "By walking away from a $60,000 commission ($0.06 per share on 1 million shares), the broker proved he had the creator's best interests at heart, building deep trust.",
  "Always use a story to capture and convey your message in presentations, speaking engagements, and pitch meetings."
 ],
 "content": "If you want to elevate your next presentation, your next pitch meeting, or just holding court at the bar — you need to become a better storyteller. Most people think their idea isn’t landing because the idea is weak. Usually the idea is fine. It’s the delivery that’s flat. [Spoken] How to be more persuasive. I can teach you to be more persuasive in 90 seconds. Okay, I get paid to speak at companies, conferences, masterminds to talk about storytelling, okay? And this is what I tell them. Data informs, story persuades. If you don't have a story, then you have no way to persuade people, right? So let's say you have a presentation and one of the points you want to make is trust is the most valuable currency in business, right? Trust is the most valuable currency in business, which totally agree, but on its own, it's kind of, it's kind of flat, right? So this is what you'd want to do, or this is what I would do. When I was a hedge fund trader, I had a new broker at Merrill Lynch, and I hadn't really used him, so I was like, you know, I just, I kind of want to test this guy out and see, see what he's like, right? So I call him up, and I give him a million shares of Biogen to buy. And he says, I'll call you back on the outside. I was like, what? He said, I'll call you back on the outside. So I was like, all right, so I hang up the wire, and my outside line starts ringing, and I pick it up, and he said, hey, my biotech trader is out today, and the backup sucks. He's like, go away. And I was like, what? He was like, go away. And I was like, I hang up the phone, and I'm like, at the time, I paid six cents a share. So on a million shares, that was a $60,000 commission check that he was walking away from. And I was like, oh my God, I'm like, I can trust this guy. He took care of me. He was looking out for my best interest. And so that's when I realized that trust is the most valuable currency in business, right? So that's, that's storytelling. If, if you don't have a story, then you have no way to persuade people. So the next time that you have a presentation or a speaking engagement or you're in a pitch meeting, you need a story to capture what you're trying to convey. [Screen text] How to be more persuasive",
